# Project 0 - System Call Tracing

This repository contains an xv6 RISC-V implementation of the `trace` system
call exercise. The `trace` user program runs another program with syscall
tracing enabled.

## Requirements

- RISC-V GNU toolchain (`riscv64-unknown-elf-*` or `riscv64-linux-gnu-*`)
- QEMU with RISC-V support (`qemu-system-riscv64`)
- `make`

## Build and run

From the repository root, run:

```sh
make clean
make qemu
```

When xv6 starts, use its `$` prompt to run the tests below. To exit QEMU, press
Ctrl+A, release the keys, then press X.

## Test `trace`

Trace only `read` (syscall number 5, so the mask is `1 << 5`, or `32`):

```sh
trace 32 grep README user/trace.c
```

The output should contain `syscall read -> ...` lines.

Trace all syscalls:

```sh
trace 2147483647 grep README user/trace.c
```

The output should include calls such as `trace`, `exec`, `open`, `read`, and
`close`. A command run without `trace` should not print trace lines:

```sh
grep README user/trace.c
```

Calling `trace` without a command prints its usage:

```sh
trace 32
```

## Implementation

The syscall mask is stored per process. `trace` sets the mask before executing
the requested command; the kernel prints the syscall name and return value for
selected calls. Child processes inherit their parent's mask, while newly
allocated process slots start with tracing disabled.
